00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.820
Language modeling is one of the most basic and important tasks in natural language processing.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.460
There's also one that RNNs do very well.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.420
In this video, you'll learn about how to build a language model using an RNN.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.500
And this will lead up to what a fun programming exercise at the end of this week,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.420
where you build a language model and use it to generate Shakespeare-like texts and other types of texts.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:25.540
Let's get started.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.860
So what is a language model?

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.620
Let's say you're building a speech recognition system and you hear the sentence,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.180
the apple and pear salad was delicious.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.300
So what did you just hear me say?

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.100
Did I say the apple and pear salad?

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.540
Or did I say the apple and pear salad?

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:49.300
You probably think the second sentence is much more likely.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.220
And in fact, that's what a good speech recognition system would output,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.420
even though these two sentences sound exactly the same.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.700
And the way a speech recognition system picks the second sentence is by using a language model,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.180
which tells it what is the probability of either of these two sentences.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.740
For example, a language model might say that the chance of the first sentence is

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.100
3.2 by 10 to the negative 13.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.860
And the chance of the second sentence is, say, 5.7 by 10 to the negative 10.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:28.500
And so with these probabilities, the second sentence is much more likely by over a factor

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.020
of 10 to the 3 compared to the first sentence.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.700
And that's why a speech recognition system would pick the second choice.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.340
So what a language model does is given any sentence,

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.580
it's job is to tell you what is the probability of a sentence, of that particular sentence.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:51.940
And by probability of sentence, I mean if you were to pick up a random newspaper,

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.660
open a random email, or pick a random web page, or listen to the next thing someone says,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:00.740
the friend of you says, what is the chance that the next sentence you read somewhere out

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:05.220
there in the world will be a particular sentence like the apple and pear salad?

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.140
And this is a fundamental component for both speech recognition systems, as you've just seen,

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.740
as well as for machine translation systems, where translation systems want to output only

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.660
sentences that are likely.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:27.140
And so the basic job of a language model is to input a sentence, which I'm going to write

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:33.540
as a sequence, y1, y2, up to yty.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:40.500
And for language model, it will be useful to represent the sentences as outputs y rather

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.700
than as inputs x.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:48.900
But what a language model does is it estimates the probability of that particular sequence of words.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:03:02.540
So how do you build a language model? To build such a model using a RNN, you would first need a

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:08.700
training set comprising a large corpus of English text, or text from whatever language you want to

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:15.260
build a language model of. And the word corpus is an NLP terminology that just means a large body

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.700
or a very large set of English text of English sentences.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:24.460
So let's say you get a sentence in your training set, as follows, cut average 15 hours of sleep a

